<PinScene>
  <CowScene />
  <Caption beat={0}>A farmer's cow stops giving milk. So he calls a physicist.</Caption>
  <Caption beat={1}>"First," she says, "assume a spherical cow."</Caption>
  <Caption beat={2}>"In a vacuum."</Caption>
  <Caption beat={3}>Ridiculous — and honest. A spherical cow is the <em>easiest</em> cow to push uphill. If even it won't move, no cow will.</Caption>
  <Caption beat={4}>The problem was never the shape. It was the weight.</Caption>
</PinScene>

## Now, the spherical human

Same trick. New subject.

<!--
  REMARK(beat 5 via shared canvas): the Musk→circle morph happens ON THE SAME canvas as the
  cow — the place stays, the occupant changes. Causal-claim guard kept in the words ("we are
  not going to explain how he got it"); brief §4. Logged in notes/draft-decisions.md (D5).
-->
<!--
  REMARK(reduction line): authored, crude, UNATTRIBUTED; "money" not "net worth" (jargon).
  See notes/draft-decisions.md D6. The area-honesty lesson rides the 'coins' beat caption.
  TEST(Study A, reader-study.md): can readers rank circles and judge rough ratios from
  area alone? Revisit if area is misread as radius.
-->
<PinScene>
  <PersonScene />
  <Caption beat={0}>Take the most-photographed fortune on earth. We won't explain how he got it — we'll do to him exactly what the physicist did to the cow.</Caption>
  <Caption beat={1}>Throw away the companies, the timing, the inheritance, the talent, the luck — every argument about whether any of it was earned.</Caption>
  <Caption beat={2}>What is left is almost offensively small.</Caption>
  <Caption beat={3}><strong>You are your money.</strong> The area is the fortune: twice as wide, four times as rich.</Caption>
  <Caption beat={4}>I want that to feel wrong. The wrongness is the point. We are building the smallest possible room — a person is a number, nothing else.</Caption>
</PinScene>

<!--
  BUILD NOTE (2026-07-06, M4): OneTrade + ScriptedRounds merged into TradeScene — one
  scrub-reversible teaching sequence. Numbers still come from the real trade rule with
  authored outcomes only (presets.ts honesty note). Two-sided coin wears the traders'
  fill colors: no heads/tails to remember, the color that lands wins (storyboard p5–6).
-->
<PinScene>
  <TradeScene />
  <Caption beat={0}>Give it company. Someone new walks in — different colors, a little poorer.</Caption>
  <Caption beat={1}>To trade, each antes the same slice: a fifth of whatever the <em>poorer</em> one owns. Nobody can stake more than they have.</Caption>
  <Caption beat={2}>Then a two-sided coin, one side each. Whichever color lands takes the pot. Nothing else decides — not size, not shape, not who "deserves" it.</Caption>
  <Caption beat={3}>A win, a loss — and everyone is roughly where they started. The winner took exactly what the loser lost. No money made, none destroyed. That is the whole game, and it is fair.</Caption>
  <Caption beat={4}>Hold on to that feeling. It is about to betray you.</Caption>
</PinScene>

## Your turn to guess

Here is the setup, clean:

- everyone starts with the same circle;
- every trade picks a random pair;
- every coin is fair, fifty-fifty.

Now let it run. Not five trades. A hundred thousand.

What does the room look like after a hundred thousand fair trades? Pick one before you
scroll.

<!--
  WIDGET: Prediction — the reader commits to an outcome before the reveal; the choice is
  quoted back by the reveal widget. Stored locally only.
-->
<Prediction />

## Now run it

<!--
  WIDGET: RevealRun mode="reveal" — the real Yard-Sale rule, tuned preset, no parameter
  controls. UNSEEDED per the storyboard ("do not use seed, let every run be different");
  median top share ≈ 45% at this preset, so the concentration claim is distributional,
  never per-run. Claim discipline: describes the finite run only — NO power-law or
  distribution-family claim here (gated, Chapter III). Asymptotics are beat 17.
-->
<RevealRun mode="reveal" />

One of them is enormous. The rest are dust.

Every trade was fair — I just showed you. No coin was loaded. Nobody cheated. Nobody was
smarter. And still: one circle ate the room.

The trade was fair. The result is not. Those are two different things, and the whole essay
lives in the gap between them.

## Don't believe it? Run it again.

<!--
  WIDGET: RevealRun mode="rerun" — new seed each run, same preset; a different agent wins.
  Each run's end state is logged so Chapter III builds its histogram from a room the reader
  actually watched.
-->
<RevealRun mode="rerun" />

Different winner. Same ending.

Run it again. And again. The crown lands on someone new every time — but it always lands.
The winner is random. The winner-take-all is not.

## So why did they win?

The room has its winner. The next morning, the news explains them.

<!--
  WIDGET: WinnerStory mode="first" — a seeded run (501) whose winner happens to hold the
  "square" trait; the headline is generated from that trait, reusing the opening
  `headline-card` motif ON PURPOSE (the essay opened on a real headline; now we manufacture
  one about a random circle). [TEST] reader-study Study D.
  GUARD (critical failure mode + widget contract): traits are assigned BEFORE the run and
  NEVER enter pair selection, the stake, or the coin. The sim core cannot read them — it has
  no import path to the trait table. Do NOT name a cognitive bias in prose (research.md C12).
-->
<WinnerStory mode="first" />

Look at them. A red pentagon with a teal edge, in a room of every other mix. Sharper. More
decisive. Built to win. The story writes itself.

And it is a lie. That trait — the color, the edge, the corners — never touched the game.
Not the coin, not who got picked, not the stake. I dealt the looks out before the first
trade, and not one of them changed a single outcome.

<!--
  WIDGET: WinnerStory mode="rerun" — run again: a different winner, a different distinctive
  trait, a new headline written with the same confidence. The story is always selected after
  the result. The reader chooses their own stopping point ("convinced, or run again?").
-->
<WinnerStory mode="rerun" />

Same room. New winner. New headline, just as convincing.

Remember the headline we started with?

Watch how easily a result invites a story.

## Line them up

A hundred circles, all different sizes, scattered around the room. You cannot read that.
Nobody can.

So sort them. Smallest to largest, and drop each one into a bin — a pile for the near-broke,
a pile for the comfortable, a pile for the rich.

<!--
  WIDGET: BinsStage — the histogram is built FROM the same circles the reader watched trade
  (the latest logged run; the seeded reveal room as fallback). Bin width is a reader-visible
  choice, not nature's.
-->
<BinsStage />

Now it is a shape: a chart whose bars are the people you just watched. (Where I cut the
lines between the piles is a choice, not nature's — move them and the picture shifts a
little.)

And the shape leans hard. A crowd of the poor piled up on one side, a thin tail reaching
out the other — and far past everyone, alone, the winner.

<!-- TEST(Study B, reader-study.md): histogram vs cumulative/Lorenz as the first distribution view; choose by comprehension, not familiarity. -->

## The picture is broken

There is a problem with that chart. The winner is so large that, to fit them on the screen,
everyone else gets crushed into one thin sliver at the bottom. Every difference among the
people who actually lost — gone.

So change the ruler. Instead of each step *adding* the same amount, each step *multiplies*:
1, 10, 100, 1,000. The same distance across the screen now means ten times the money.

<!--
  WIDGET: AxisStage — the same people glide from their linear spots to logarithmic ones; the
  crushed sliver opens into a readable spread. The truly empty sit in their own pile, off the
  ruler.
-->
<AxisStage />

Now you can see all of them at once. And the gap between two circles stops telling you how
much more one has. It tells you how many *times* more.

(One honest catch: you cannot multiply your way up from zero, so a circle with nothing has
no spot on this ruler. The truly empty sit in their own pile, off to the side.)

<!--
  GATE(no power law): the log view only reveals values a linear scale hid. It does NOT show or
  prove a power-law / heavy-tailed distribution family — omit "power law" from the baseline arc
  entirely (research.md; narrative-sources.md log note). A fit + goodness-of-fit + model
  comparison would be required first.
  TEST(Study C, reader-study.md): does a side-by-side linear↔log transform plus a comprehension
  check leave readers able to read ratios? A bare label is not enough (Ryan & Evers).
-->

## One number for the whole room

A histogram is a picture. Sometimes you need a single number instead — to compare two
rooms, or to watch one room drift.

<!--
  WIDGET: GiniStage — the Gini coefficient built FROM the same circles the reader watched
  (latest logged run; reveal-room fallback): line up, accumulate (Lorenz), equal line,
  shaded gap. Same build-it-from-the-people animation style as the histogram (review.md:
  "we can do the same for gini"). Beat 21 discipline: introduced only for comparing whole
  rooms; top share stays beside it; no per-trade Gini claims (C5).
-->
<GiniStage />

Zero: everyone holds the same. One: a single owner and everyone else at nothing. This room
already speaks for itself — and now it can be compared with any other room, later, with one
number.

## More of them

You have seen it with a handful. Watch it with a crowd.

<!--
  WIDGET: CrowdRun — a much larger seeded population (1,000 agents, 2M trades) runs the same
  fair trades; the log-ruled histogram forms live.
-->
<CrowdRun />

Same rules. Same fair coin. Many more people. Read it yourself — the wall of the poor, the
thinning tail, the one circle out past the edge. The crowd does not soften the result. It
sharpens it.

## Where it ends

So where does this go, if no one ever stops it?

This part is a theorem, not just an animation. Keep the room finite. Keep every trade fair,
every coin even. Then let it run — not a hundred thousand trades, but without end.

<!--
  WIDGET: TimeLapse — time accelerates; the tail thins until one circle holds everything the
  pixels can show. Picks up the reveal room where it stopped; a fresh room ends the same way
  with a different owner.
-->
<TimeLapse />

With probability one, it ends with a single owner. Everyone else goes to zero. A different
winner each time you run it — but always, in the end, exactly one.

Two cautions, because they carry the honesty of the whole piece:

- "Without end" is doing the work. This is the limit, not a date. For any real number of
  trades you get deep concentration — not literally one owner. The time-lapse shows a
  destination, not a deadline.
- The path can wobble. A poorer circle can win, and the room can briefly grow fairer, even
  as the whole process grinds toward the one.

<aside class="claim-note">
  <p>The finite-agent convergence result is proved in <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.01485">Börgers &amp; Greengard (2023)</a>.</p>
</aside>

## Your hand on the dial

So that is the game as written: fair coin, fixed stake, one ending.

Now you get a knob.

The stake — the slice each trade puts on the table — has been fixed this whole time. Take
hold of it. Turn it down, and each trade risks almost nothing. Turn it up, and each trade is
a gamble for a real chunk.

<!--
  WIDGET: StakeDial — dragging the stake re-runs the room live at the chosen value; each
  stake replays a fixed seed, so the comparison is repeatable.
  GATE(β timing): the qualitative split is safe — asymptotic concentration is the same for
  every fixed β in (0,1) (C4); the *rate* is shown as what the live runs do, not a precise
  law. Do NOT state a precise timing relationship (e.g. a formula in β or N) until the
  confirmatory grid across N, β, seeds lands (research/baseline.md). The widget's own
  footnote says "what these runs did — not a law."
-->
<StakeDial />

Watch what changes — and what refuses to.

Turn the stake down and the collapse drags: the room looks fair for a long, comfortable
while. Turn it up and the winner arrives fast and brutal. The speed is yours.

The ending is not. Any stake above nothing, given enough time, still empties into a single
circle. You can change how fast the room falls. You do not get to vote on whether it falls.

(Set the stake to exactly zero and the room freezes — but only because then nobody is
trading at all. Nothing at stake, nothing happens.)

## Now you try to stop it

Reading about it is one thing. Try to fix it yourself.

Here is a live room, falling the usual way. Your job: keep it from collapsing. Your tool is
blunt and satisfying — point at a circle that has grown too fat, and tax it. Whatever you
pull out is shared back across the room.

<!--
  WIDGET: TaxGame — the live room keeps trading while the reader taps; tapping a circle
  levies a quarter of its current wealth and shares it back equally; the tapped circle
  flinches (display affect only). This is a manual WEALTH levy (taxes current wealth, not
  income) — named in the widget copy, C13.
  DESIGN GUARD honored: the pace is NOT tuned so the reader provably cannot win — a diligent
  hand genuinely can hold the room (robot-player tuning notes in the build log). Whether a
  fast enough hand can keep a room fair is left as the real, open question the prose asks.
-->
<TaxGame />

Go ahead. Chase the big ones. Tap, tap, tap.

Notice how it feels. The room keeps trading while you work — you tax one giant, and two more
are already swelling behind your back. It is like bailing a boat that refills itself.

Maybe you can hold it. Maybe you cannot. That is genuinely worth finding out, so I will not
pretend to know the answer for you: can a fast enough hand keep a room fair?

<!--
  OPEN(outline beat 19): where the taxed wealth goes is an unresolved design fork — split evenly
  to everyone vs. handed to whoever is poorest. Different mechanisms, different lessons. The
  widget implements the split-evenly reading the prose describes ("shared back across the
  room"); the parenthetical below flags the fork without deciding it.
-->
(There is a quieter question hiding in here, too: the money you take — should it be split
evenly across everyone, or handed straight to whoever has least? Those are different choices,
and they teach different lessons. Hold that thought.)

