## Put the levy in the rules

Your hands were always going to tire. So take you out of it: make the levy part of the
room's physics, not a hero's reflexes.

First, see the tool alone — freeze the trading.

<TaxOnlyDemo />

## Tax against trade

Now both rules run at once: every trade pushes the room toward one owner, every levy pulls
it back toward equal. Who wins depends on the two dials. So let's stop running one room at
a time and run <em>all of them</em>.

<!--
  WIDGET: PhaseDiagram — 10×13 grid of (stake β, flat levy per round), each cell the tail-mean
  Gini of a 200k-trade room, two seeds averaged, computed live in-page from the same engine
  (chunked under one frame budget; cached per session). White contour = MEASURED Gini 0.5.
  Dashed curve = FITTED τ* ≈ c·β² (calibration + fit: scripts/phase-calibrate.ts and
  research/phase.ts; stability notes in notes/research/interventions.md). GATE discipline:
  "fitted", never "theoretical"/"law".
-->
<PhaseDiagram />

One map, two regimes. Below the frontier, the stake outruns the levy and the room slides
toward its single owner — slower, but surely. Above it, the levy holds. And the frontier
itself is not a straight trade-off: double the stake and you need roughly <em>four times</em>
the tax to stand still.

## The sandbox

Everything you have seen was this one machine wearing different costumes. Here it is with
every dial exposed: people, stake, interest, a flat levy or a progressive one you shape
yourself. Break it. Save it. See what it takes.

<!--
  WIDGET: Sandbox — the "all bells and whistles" finale (review.md). SandboxWorld composes
  engine primitives (shares sum 1 + totalDollars scalar; brackets in DOLLARS; interest
  scales the total, not the shares — mechanism note in notes/research/extensions.md).
  Beat-22 discipline: the progressive designer is a TOY — no empirical claim is made about
  real tax design (Lima/Vieira/Anteneodo gate still open).
-->
<Sandbox />

## So — merit, or math?

<!--
  BUILD NOTE (2026-07-06, M10): the ending is a PinScene bookending the opening — the lone
  winner, the honesty bill (the discarded world drifting back), the headline echoed in the
  teletype mono, then bare paper and the closing line. Beats 24–25.
-->
<PinScene>
  <ClosingScene />
  <Caption beat={0}>This is where every fair room ends, given time. Before the last word — the honesty bill.</Caption>
  <Caption beat={1}>To build this room I threw away work, wages, debt, inheritance, skill, effort, luck, power, law — and never handed them back. That list is the fence around the claim: a model this bare cannot explain real inequality, and it never tried to.</Caption>
  <Caption beat={2}>I still cannot tell you how he got there — this little room does not know his name. But it shows something smaller and harder: a process can be fair in every single step and still end with everything in one place. So when the stories arrive — the talent, the grind, the vision — maybe they're real. Or maybe you are reading a winning trait off a fair coin.</Caption>
  <Caption beat={3}><strong>Nothing was unfair. That was the unfairness.</strong></Caption>
</PinScene>

